---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

import viril1 from '../../assets/images/projects/viril/viril1.png';
import viril2 from '../../assets/images/projects/viril/viril2.png';
import viril3 from '../../assets/images/projects/viril/viril3.png';
import viril4 from '../../assets/images/projects/viril/viril4.png';
import viril5 from '../../assets/images/projects/viril/viril5.png';
import viril6 from '../../assets/images/projects/viril/viril6.png';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Viril - Artem Androsov"
  description="Viril — a men's health brand built from zero. Brand strategy, naming, UX/UI, and 64 screens across four versions."
  ogImage="/og/viril.jpg"
  activePage="projects"
>
        <div class="back-nav">
            <a href="/" class="back-link">Back to projects</a>
        </div>

        <section class="project-hero" id="hero">
            <div class="section-label">Case study</div>
            <h1>Viril</h1>
            <p class="project-brief">A men's health brand for the European market — built from a blank page in five
                weeks. The core product challenge wasn't visual: it was how to guide a user who doesn't know what he
                needs toward the right product without losing his trust along the way.</p>

            <div class="project-meta">
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="meta-label">Date</span>
                    <span class="meta-value">October 2024</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="meta-label">My role</span>
                    <span class="meta-value">Product, Brand Strategy, UX/UI</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="meta-label">Client</span>
                    <span class="meta-value">EU Men's Health Brand</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meta-item">
                    <span class="meta-label">Areas</span>
                    <div class="meta-tags">
                        <span class="meta-tag">Brand Strategy</span>
                        <span class="meta-tag">Naming</span>
                        <span class="meta-tag">UX/UI</span>
                        <span class="meta-tag">Figma</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="project-concept" id="concept">
            <div class="www-grid">
                <div class="www-column">
                    <h2 class="www-title">Why</h2>
                    <p class="www-text">The client came with a product — men's health supplements for the European
                        market — and no brand. The niche has a well-documented failure pattern: brands either go
                        clinical and cold, or tabloid and cheap. Neither builds the trust required for a purchase this
                        personal. I mapped three direct competitors — Hims, Roman, and BlueChew — documenting their
                        full user journey: entry point, trust signals, product presentation, checkout friction. The
                        finding was consistent: every brand assumes the user already knows what he needs. Most don't.
                        That gap wasn't a messaging problem — it was a product architecture problem.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="www-column">
                    <h2 class="www-title">What</h2>
                    <p class="www-text">I ran the full cycle solo: competitive analysis → positioning strategy → naming
                        → brand voice → visual system → UX architecture → UI in Figma. The centrepiece was a
                        7-question recommendation quiz — a guided flow that surfaces the right product based on what
                        actually matters: tolerance to side effects, desired duration, need for ejaculation control,
                        and whether the user plans ahead or acts spontaneously. The quiz replaces self-diagnosis with
                        guided decision-making. Beyond the quiz: 64 screens across four versions (EN/ES × desktop/mobile),
                        covering the full user journey from landing to post-purchase. Naming, brand platform, and every
                        screen — owned end to end.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="www-column">
                    <h2 class="www-title">How</h2>
                    <p class="www-text">Competitive analysis came first — three brands, every screen, every trust signal
                        documented. That became the filter: if something was already broken in the market, I wasn't
                        repeating it. The quiz architecture required working backwards from product logic: understanding
                        which variables actually differentiate the right supplement choice, then building a question flow
                        that surfaces those without feeling like a medical intake form. Before handoff, I ran usability
                        sessions with 5 men — testing comprehension of quiz questions and flow clarity. Feedback led to
                        simplifying two questions and reordering the sequence. The client approved the UX architecture
                        on the first pass — zero structural revisions. Timeline: 3 days research · 1 week brand · 2
                        weeks UX · 2 weeks UI.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="project-gallery" id="gallery">
            <div class="section-label">Process &amp; outcome</div>
            <h2>What it looks like</h2>

            <div class="gallery-grid">
                <div class="gallery-item">
                    <Image src={viril1} alt="Viril — image 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </div>
                <div class="gallery-item">
                    <Image src={viril2} alt="Viril — image 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </div>
                <div class="gallery-item">
                    <Image src={viril3} alt="Viril — image 3" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </div>
                <div class="gallery-item">
                    <Image src={viril4} alt="Viril — image 4" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </div>
                <div class="gallery-item">
                    <Image src={viril5} alt="Viril — image 5" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </div>
                <div class="gallery-item">
                    <Image src={viril6} alt="Viril — image 6" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="project-results" id="results">
            <div class="section-label">Results</div>
            <h2>What we delivered</h2>

            <div class="metrics-grid">
                <div class="metric-card">
                    <span class="metric-number">64</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Screens</span>
                    <p class="metric-description">Full product: 16 screens × EN/ES × desktop/mobile</p>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card">
                    <span class="metric-number">5 weeks</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Full cycle</span>
                    <p class="metric-description">Research → strategy → naming → brand → UX → UI</p>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card">
                    <span class="metric-number">7</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Quiz questions</span>
                    <p class="metric-description">Each question maps to a clinical variable — side effects, duration,
                        control, spontaneity — determining the exact product recommendation</p>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card">
                    <span class="metric-number">0</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">UX revisions</span>
                    <p class="metric-description">Architecture approved first pass — visual edits only</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="project-shipped" id="shipped">
            <div class="section-label">What shipped</div>
            <p class="shipped-text">The product launched and is live. The recommendation quiz, user flows, and brand
                voice transferred to production intact. The final build — assembled on WordPress — diverges from the
                original Figma spec in visual precision: a constraint common when clients move to template-based
                implementation post-handoff. The UX architecture and product logic held.
            </p>
            <a href="https://viril.live" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display: inline-block; margin-top: 12px; color: inherit; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 500;">View live site →</a>
        </section>

        <section class="next-project" id="next">
            <div class="section-label">Next project</div>
            <a href="/projects/talent" class="next-project-link">
                <span class="next-project-name">Talent</span>
                <span class="next-project-arrow">→</span>
            </a>
        </section>

        <section class="cta-section" id="cta">
            <h2>Starting from zero?</h2>
            <p class="contact-subtitle contact-subtitle-margin">
                A blank page is where I do my best work. Tell me about the product.
            </p>
            <a href="https://t.me/androsovart" target="_blank" class="btn-primary" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get in
                touch</a>
        </section>

        <Footer />
</ProjectLayout>
